    "## 19. Navigation Observations to Investigate\n",
    "\n",
    "The following observations are proposed for evaluation using simulated experiments. They are not presented as measured experimental results unless the corresponding experiments are executed.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Observation 1 — Not Every Discovered Obstacle Causes Replanning\n",
    "\n",
    "A newly discovered obstacle should only cause replanning when it affects the current planned route. Obstacles located away from the current route may not require a new path.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Observation 2 — Maze Structure Can Affect Replanning\n",
    "\n",
    "Mazes containing narrow passages, branches and alternative routes may produce different replanning behaviour compared with simpler maze structures.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Observation 3 — Partial Observation Can Increase Travel Distance\n",
    "\n",
    "When the robot does not know the complete maze, it may initially follow a route that later becomes invalid. This can cause additional movement compared with navigation using complete map information.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Observation 4 — Larger Mazes Can Increase Navigation Complexity\n",
    "\n",
    "As maze size increases, the number of cells and possible routes increases. This can increase the amount of information that must be discovered and the planning effort required.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Observation 5 — Visual Complexity Does Not Directly Determine Replanning Count\n",
    "\n",
